hadiths[46]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1680","Abdur Rahman bin Abu Bakrah narrated from his father that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""A judge or arbitrator should not decide between two people while he is angry."""
"1681","Abdur Rahman bin Abi Bakrah narrated from his father that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""A ruler or judge should not decide between two people while he is angry."""
"1682","It is narrated from Zuhri that Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""I have not seen anyone consult his companions more than the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)."" Imam Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy on him) said: And Allah the Exalted said: ""And their affairs are (decided) by mutual consultation among themselves."" (__QUR_N__ Ash-Shura: 38)"
"1683","It is narrated from Salim that Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) returned with the people based on the hadith of Abdur Rahman bin Awf (may Allah be pleased with him), which he narrated from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) regarding not entering a plague-stricken area—that is, when he (Umar) had set out for Syria (from Madinah) and received news of the outbreak of plague there."
"1684","It is narrated from Abu Ja'far Muhammad bin Ali that when Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) organized the registers (for distributing stipends), he said: ""In your opinion, with whom should I begin?"" He was advised: ""Start with your closest relatives."" He said: ""Rather, I will begin with the closest relatives of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)."""
"1685","It is narrated from Amr bin Al-Aas (may Allah be pleased with him) that he heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saying: ""When a ruler exercises ijtihad (independent judgment) and his decision is correct, he will have a double reward; and when a ruler exercises ijtihad and makes a mistake, he will have a single reward."""
"1686","Abdul Aziz bin Muhammad narrated to us from Yazid bin Hadi, he said: I narrated this hadith to Abu Bakr bin Muhammad bin Amr bin Hazm, so he said: ""Abu Salamah bin Abdur Rahman narrated to me from Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) in the same manner."""
"1687","It is narrated from Amr bin Al-Aas (may Allah be pleased with him) that he heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saying: ""When a ruler makes a judgment by ijtihad and is correct, he will have a double reward, and when a ruler makes a judgment by ijtihad and errs, he will have a single reward."""
"1688","Yazid bin Hād said: ""I narrated this hadith to Abu Bakr bin Muhammad bin Amr bin Hazm, so he said that Abu Salamah narrated this hadith to me in the same way from Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him)."""
"1689","Umm al-Mu’minin Sayyidah Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: “Indeed, I am a human being, and you bring your disputes to me. It is possible that one of you may be more eloquent in presenting his case than the other, and I judge in his favor based on what I hear. So, if I give someone a right that belongs to his brother, then he should not take it, for in that way I am allotting for him a piece of the Hellfire.”"
"1690","It is narrated from Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Indeed, I am only a human being like you, and you bring your disputes to me. It may be that some of you are more eloquent in presenting their case than others, so I judge in their favor based on what I hear. So, if I judge in someone's favor at the expense of his brother's right, then he should not take it, for in that case I am allotting for him a piece of the Hellfire."""
"1691","It is narrated from Haram bin Sa'd bin Muhisah that the she-camel of Bara' bin Azib (may Allah be pleased with him) entered the garden of a people and destroyed it. So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) gave the verdict that during the day, the protection of property is the responsibility of the owners, and if livestock cause damage at night, then their owner is liable for it. (That is, the loss occurring at night will be taken from the owner of the animal.)"
"1692","It is narrated from Haram bin Muhayyisa, who narrated from Bara bin Azib (may Allah be pleased with him), that the she-camel of Bara bin Azib (may Allah be pleased with him) entered the garden of an Ansari and destroyed it. So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) gave the verdict that it is obligatory upon the owners of the gardens to guard them during the day, and whatever damage the cattle cause at night, its compensation is upon the owners of the cattle."
"1693","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""The burden of proof is upon the claimant."" (The narrator says) I think he also said, though I do not remember it with certainty, that he (the Prophet) also said: ""(In the absence of witnesses) the oath is upon the defendant."""
"1694","It is narrated from Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both) that two men claimed ownership of an animal, and each of them presented testimony that the animal belonged to him and that he had raised (and bred) it. So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) decided in favor of the one in whose possession the animal was."
"1695","It is narrated from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) that when he doubted his son, he called a physiognomist for him."
"1696","It is narrated from Yahya bin Abdur Rahman bin Hatib that two men claimed a child, so Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) called for those skilled in physiognomy. They said, ""Both of them share in him."" Then Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said to the child, ""Go to whichever of them you wish."""
"1697","Through another chain, from Sulaiman bin Yasar, a narration with the same meaning as the previous hadith is reported from Umar (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"1698","Through another chain, it is also narrated with the same meaning from Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) through the authority of Urwah bin Zubair."
"1699","It is narrated from Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that Sa'd (bin 'Ubadah, may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)! If I find a man with my wife, should I wait until I bring four witnesses?"" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Yes."""
"1700","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him) asked: ""O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)! If I find a man with my wife, should I wait until I bring four witnesses?"" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) replied: ""Yes."""
"1701","It is narrated from Ibn Musayyib that Ali bin Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) was asked about a man who found another man with his wife and killed him, or killed his wife, so he said: ""If he does not bring four witnesses, then he should be prepared to be killed himself."""
"1702","It is narrated from Sa'id bin al-Musayyib that in Syria, a man found another man with his wife and killed him, or he killed the woman. So (the governor of Syria) Mu'awiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, may Allah be pleased with him, that you should ask this matter for them from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him. He asked Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and he said: ""This incident did not happen in the land of Iraq. I adjure you to speak the truth."" Then he told the reality, so Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""I am Abu al-Hasan. If he does not bring four witnesses, then he should be content with being killed."""
"1703","Sufyan bin ‘Uyaynah (may Allah have mercy on him) narrated that I heard al-Zuhri (may Allah have mercy on him) say: The people of Iraq think that the testimony of one who accuses others (of adultery) is not permissible. And I bear witness that Sa‘id bin al-Musayyib (may Allah have mercy on him) informed me that ‘Umar bin al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) said to Abu Bakrah (may Allah be pleased with him): “Repent, and your testimony will be accepted, or if you repent, your testimony will be accepted.” Imam al-Shafi‘i (may Allah have mercy on him) said: I heard Sufyan bin ‘Uyaynah (may Allah have mercy on him) narrate it several times in this way, then I heard him say that he had some doubt about it. Imam al-Shafi‘i (may Allah have mercy on him) said: I bear witness that so-and-so informed me—then he mentioned a man whose name I could not remember, so I asked him, and ‘Umar bin Qays informed me that he narrates from Sa‘id bin al-Musayyib. And Sufyan (may Allah have mercy on him) did not have any doubt regarding Sa‘id bin al-Musayyib. Imam al-Shafi‘i (may Allah have mercy on him) said: And besides him, no one else narrates it from Ibn Shihab except Sa‘id bin al-Musayyib (may Allah have mercy on him), and he narrates from ‘Umar (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"1704","Sufyan bin ‘Uyaynah informed us, he said: Zuhri informed me. Then when we stood up (from the gathering), ‘Umar bin Qais, who was present with me in the gathering, said to me that he is Sa‘id bin al-Musayyib. I asked Sufyan (may Allah have mercy on him): “Did you have doubt when Sa‘id bin al-Musayyib (may Allah have mercy on him) informed you?” He replied: “No, it is exactly as he said, but he made me doubtful.”"
"1705","It is narrated from Sa'id bin al-Musayyib (may Allah have mercy on him) that when 'Umar bin al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) enforced the hadd of qadhf on three men, he made them repent. Two of them repented, so he accepted their testimony, but Abu Bakrah (may Allah be pleased with him) refused to repent, so his testimony was also rejected."
"1706","It is narrated from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata’ (may Allah have mercy on him), that he said regarding accepting the testimony of women in matters concerning women: ""The testimony of less than four is not permissible in this."""
"1707","It is narrated from Ata (may Allah have mercy on him) that he said: ""In matters concerning women, the testimony of women, when there is no man with them, is not permissible with less than four just women."""
"1708","It is narrated from Ibn Abi Mulaykah that Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) said regarding the testimony of children: ""It is not permissible."" Ibn Jurayj narrated from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him), with these additional words: ""Because Allah the Exalted says: 'Those whom you approve of as witnesses.' (__QUR_N__ al-Baqarah: 282)"""
"1709","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) gave a verdict based on an oath and one witness."
"1710","It is narrated from Mu'adh bin Abdur Rahman that he narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) and another man whose name he mentioned (but I do not remember his name now), who was among the companions of the Prophet ﷺ, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave a verdict based on an oath and one witness."
"1711","It is narrated from Ibn Musayyib (may Allah have mercy on him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) gave a verdict in a case based on an oath and one witness."
"1712","It is narrated from Saeed bin Amr bin Shurahbeel bin Saeed bin Saad bin Ubadah, from his father, and he from his grandfather, that he said: ""We found written in the books of Saad (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) gave a judgment with an oath and one witness."""
"1713","Amr bin Shurahbil narrated that we found in the books of Sa'd bin Ubadah (may Allah be pleased with him), Sa'd bin Ubadah (may Allah be pleased with him) testifies that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) ordered Amr bin Hazm (may Allah be pleased with him) to judge with an oath and one witness."
"1714","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) gave a verdict based on an oath and one witness."
"1715","Ja'far bin Muhammad (may Allah have mercy on him) narrated from his father that the Prophet ﷺ gave a verdict based on an oath and one witness."
"1716","Ja'far bin Muhammad (may Allah have mercy on him) narrated that I heard Hakam bin 'Utaybah asking my father while he was standing with his hand on the wall of a grave: (He asked) ""Did the Prophet ﷺ give a judgment with an oath and one witness?"" He replied: ""Yes, and likewise 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) gave a judgment among you."""
"1717","Amr bin Shu'aib narrated from his father that the Prophet ﷺ said regarding testimony: ""If (the claimant) brings one witness, then he should also swear an oath along with the witness (then the decision will be in his favor)."""
"1718","It is narrated from Abu Ja'far (may Allah have mercy on him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) gave a verdict based on an oath and one witness."
"1719","It is narrated from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace be upon him) gave a verdict based on an oath and one witness."
"1720","It is narrated from Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever swears a false oath upon this pulpit of mine has made his abode in Hell."""
"1721","Abu Ghatfan Al-Mari narrated that Zaid bin Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him) and Ibn Muti’ brought a dispute about a (shared) house before Marwan bin Hakam. Marwan ruled that Zaid bin Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him) should take an oath on the pulpit. Zaid (may Allah be pleased with him) said: “I will take the oath at my place.” Marwan said: “No, by Allah! Take the oath where people’s cases are decided (i.e., on the pulpit).” So Zaid (may Allah be pleased with him) would take the oath that he was truthful, but refused to take the oath on the pulpit, and Marwan was astonished at this. Imam Malik (may Allah have mercy on him) said that Zaid (may Allah be pleased with him) disliked being forced to take an oath."
"1722","Ibn Abi Mulaykah narrated that I wrote to Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) regarding two slave girls from Ta’if, one of whom had struck the other, and at that time there was no witness. So he wrote back to me in reply: Detain them after Asr and recite to them this verse: “Those who exchange Allah’s covenant and their oaths for a small price...” (__QUR_N__: Aal-e-Imran: 77). Ibn Abi Mulaykah says: I did as he instructed, and one of them confessed (to the crime)."
"1723","Ata narrated: I and Ubaid bin Umair went to Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) while she was observing i'tikaf at the place of Shaheer. I asked her about Allah's statement: ""Allah will not call you to account for your unintentional oaths"" (__QUR_N__: Al-Baqarah: 225). She replied: ""These are (the oaths which people utter habitually, such as) 'No, by Allah!' or 'Yes, by Allah!'"""
"1724","Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) narrated that a vain oath is when a person (habitually) says: ""No, by Allah!"" and ""Yes, by Allah!"""
"1725","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he said: ""Whoever swears an oath and then affirms it, then (upon breaking it) it is obligatory for him to free a slave."""
